{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2306 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "269caddb46ce8cfa4dcd2758b60beffe21cb566a9c82447499a5ab464ab308ae" :edb/db-before-t 2305 :edb/db-after-t 2306 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535618 50 #inst "2026-10-09T03:44:00.564Z" 13194139535618 true] [17592186164927 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357738 13194139535618 true] [17592186166504 1100 "Let $F(x)=x^3 - x + 5\\cos x$ for $[MATH: x\\in[0,\\pi),]$ and let $f(x)$ be the **even** extension of $F(x)$ to $x \\in (-\\pi,\\pi)$. What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in(-\\pi,0)$?" 13194139535618 false] [17592186166504 1100 "Let $F(x)=x^3 - x + 5\\cos x$ for $x\\in[0,\\pi),$ and let $f(x)$ be the **even** extension of $F(x)$ to $x \\in (-\\pi,\\pi).$ What is $f(x)$ for $x\\in(-\\pi,0)?$" 13194139535618 true] [17592186166504 1145 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $[MATH: x\\in[0,\\pi),]$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n$[MATH: {F(x), \\mid x ∈ [0, π),; F(-x), \\mid x ∈ (- π, 0).]$\n\nIn our cases, for $x\\in(-\\pi,0)$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= F(-x) \\\\\n&= (-x)^{3} - (-x) + 5\\cos (-x) \\\\\n&=-x^{3} + x + 5\\cos x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535618 false] [17592186166504 1145 "If $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in[0,\\pi),$ the even extension of $F$ is the function $f$ such that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068558/assets/766b5034dc8f2dd6f3eb8e87f22f5156f58e9ab3aaebd90fa8770d6912d46e67.png)\n\nIn our cases, for $x\\in(-\\pi,0),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =F(-x) \\\\  & =(-x)^{3}-(-x)+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}+x+5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535618 true] [17592186166505 1100 "Let $F(x)=-6x-2$ for $[MATH: x\\in[0,\\pi).]$ Find the missing parts in the Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x)$.\n\n$XX_{X}^{X}$" 13194139535618 false] [17592186166505 1100 "Let $F(x)=-6x-2$ for $x\\in[0,\\pi).$ Find the missing parts in the Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of the function $F(x).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14068558/assets/ee53aca390d2803fa92f3239dc7548792c6be9e4c05a102fe936aeb9e7ced6d6.png)" 13194139535618 true] [17592186166505 1145 "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\na_n=\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi f(x)\\cos(nx)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nf(x)=-6x-2\n$$\n\nfor $x \\in (0,\\pi)$. So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{2}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi (-6x-2) \\cos(nx)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u =-6x - 2 & ⇒u^{′} =-6 \\\\ v^{′} = \\cos (nx) & ⇒v = \\frac{1}{n}\\sin (nx)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} &= \\left.\\frac{2}{π}(uv\\right|_{0}^{π} - \\int_{0}^{π}vu^{′}dx) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{π}(\\frac{1}{n}[(-6x - 2)\\sin (nx)]_{0}^{π} + \\frac{6}{n}\\int_{0}^{π}\\sin (nx)dx) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{nπ}(((-6π - 2)\\sin (nπ) + 2\\sin (0)) - \\frac{6}{n}[\\cos (nx)]_{0}^{π}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{nπ}(0 - \\frac{6}{n}(\\cos (nπ) - \\cos (0))) \\\\\n&= (12(1 - \\cos (nπ)))/(n^{2}π)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since\n\n$$\n\\cos(n\\pi)=(-1)^n\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{12(1-(-1)^n)}{\\pi n^2}\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier cosine series is\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim (-2-3\\pi) + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\dfrac{12(1-(-1)^n)}{\\pi n^2}} \\cdot \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\cos(nx)}\n$$" 13194139535618 false] [17592186166505 1145 "For our Fourier cosine series, the coefficients are given by\n\n$$\na_n=\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi f(x)\\cos(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=-6x-2$ for $x \\in (0,\\pi).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{2}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi (-6x-2) \\cos(nx)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the integration by parts: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=-6x-2\\, & ⇒\\,{u}^{′}=-6 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)\\, & ⇒\\,v=\\frac{1}{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(uv\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi }-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }(\\frac{1}{n}[(-6x-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }+\\frac{6}{n}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(((-6\\pi -2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi )+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))-\\frac{6}{n}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)]_{0}^{\\pi }) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{n\\pi }(0-\\frac{6}{n}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))) \\\\  & =\\frac{12(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi ))}{{n}^{2}\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\cos(n\\pi)=(-1)^n,$ we have\n\n$$\na_n = \\dfrac{12(1-(-1)^n)}{\\pi n^2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the Fourier cosine series is\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim (-2-3\\pi) + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\dfrac{12(1-(-1)^n)}{\\pi n^2}} \n\\cdot \n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\cos(nx)}.\n$$" 13194139535618 true] [17592186166506 1100 "$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos(nx)\n$$\n\nLet $F(x)=\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3x}{4}\\right)$ for $x\\in[0,\\pi).$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for $a_0.$" 13194139535618 true] [17592186166506 1100 "$f(x) \\sim \\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos(nx)$\nLet $F(x)=\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3x}{4}\\right)$ for $[MATH: x\\in[0,\\pi).]$ The Fourier cosine series of the **even** $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is shown above. Find the expression for $a_0$." 13194139535618 false] [17592186166506 1145 "For our Fourier cosine series, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\na_0=\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3x}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nfor $x \\in (0,\\pi)$. So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{0} &= \\frac{2}{π}\\int_{0}^{π}\\sin (\\frac{3x}{4})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{π}[- \\frac{4}{3}\\cos (\\frac{3x}{4})]_{0}^{π} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{8}{3π}(\\cos (\\frac{3π}{4}) - \\cos (0)) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{8}{3π}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} - 1) \\\\\n&= 4 3 π (2 + 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535618 false] [17592186166506 1145 "For our Fourier cosine series, the constant term is given by\n\n$$\na_0=\\frac{2}{\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3x}{4}\\right)$ for $x \\in (0,\\pi).$ So, substituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }{[-\\frac{4}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4})]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3\\pi }(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\pi }(\\sqrt{2}+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535618 true] [17592186166889 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357739 13194139535618 true] [17592186166889 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357746 13194139535618 true] [17592186166889 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357753 13194139535618 true] [17592186166889 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357756 13194139535618 true] [17592186166890 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357811 13194139535618 true] [17592186166890 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357814 13194139535618 true] [17592186166890 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357817 13194139535618 true] [17592186166891 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357760 13194139535618 true] [17592186166891 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357767 13194139535618 true] [17592186166891 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357778 13194139535618 true] [17592186166891 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357789 13194139535618 true] [17592186166891 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357800 13194139535618 true] [17592186167223 1017 #edb/ref 17592186164903 13194139535618 false] [17592186167223 1017 #edb/ref 17592186357732 13194139535618 true] [17592186167223 1091 #edb/ref 1759218604